 "notes": "All 494 idx values (0-493) are placed, each in exactly one of 214 stations. The 'unknown' region rows (the 1883 and 1884 lists) matched existing stations by name and neighbours.\n\nMost uncertain, please check:\n1. Goodwin L.V. = North Sand Head L.V. The two never appear in the same list and they share a place and a number. Merged.\n2. Great Cotes (1880, 48b), Northrepps (1880, 55a) and the second 1880 'Teesmouth' (44b) are untyped extra rows, each sharing a number with a light-vessel. Each is kept as its own station with kind 'other'. Great Cotes may be the Bull L.V. under a shore address.\n3. Milford: High and Low have their own rows in 1880-81; from 1884 one 'Milford' / 'Milford (St Ann's)' row replaces them. That row is kept as a third group (st-anns-head) because I can't tell which light it is. Nash, by contrast, keeps 'Nash (E.)', so the plain 'Nash' goes with East/High.\n4. The joined 'Skerryvore and Hynish Signal Tower' row (1885-87) is put with Skerryvore; Hynish keeps its 1879-84 rows.\n5. Maidens: the single row up to 1886 goes with 'Maidens, North' (same no. 14); 'Maidens, South' (14*, 1887) is separate.\n6. Calf Rock (1881) and Dursey Island (1882 on, same no. 40) are kept apart as different lights; merge them if you want the station's run unbroken. Copeland is grouped across its 1884 move to Mew Island.\n7. Types printed against the evidence of position and the other years, treated as slips: Leman and Ower 'L.H.' (1887), Breaksea 'L.H.' (1884-85), Langness/'Longness' 'L.V.' (1882), Tuskar 'Lt.-ship' (1883).\n8. Misprints merged: Bona → Rona, Kirk Bank → Kish Bank, North Uist → North Unst, St. Judwall → St Tudwal's, Conan Ferry → Corran Ferry, Rhinns of May → Rhinns of Islay, 'Arran Island, Is.' → Aran North, 'Straw Island, S.' (1882, no. 34) → Aran South, Bahama Rock → Bahama Bank.\n9. Not real lighthouses at the time, kind 'other': Fair Isle (no light before 1892), Faroe, Iceland and the 'Do.' second Iceland station. Lundy Fog Gun Station, North Stack (fog signal) and Hynish Signal Tower are also 'other'.\n10. Names to check: Allonby (1881 L.H., no known light there); 'Lough Swilly' taken as Fanad Head; 'Killybegs' as Rotten Island; 'Dungarvan' as Ballinacourty; 'Lamlash' as Holy Isle; Malmö's kind.\n11. Happisburgh light and light-vessel are split only by the printed type (L.H. vs L.V.). In 1881 and 1883 the vessel and light are printed in different orders, so check the types against the scans if anything looks odd.\n12. Point of Ayr in Flintshire ('Air') and Point of Ayre in the Isle of Man are separate stations. So are Start Point in Orkney and in Devon, and Spurn Point lighthouse and the Spurn (Newsand) L.V.",
